WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.580
To Na'vi, or not to Na'vi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:08.160
that is the question. "Avatar 2" is much&nbsp;
deeper than its surface-level thrills.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.120
"Let's get it done."

00:00:10.308 --> 00:00:15.600
It's safe to say that "Avatar" is a far cry&nbsp;
from James Cameron's days directing "Piranha II:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:22.140
The Spawning." Not only was the 2009 film's&nbsp;
success historic due to its box office haul, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:27.300
it also featured new and groundbreaking special&nbsp;
effects and 3D technology, which were evident in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.340
the world of Pandora. It felt like a world none of&nbsp;
us had ever seen before, with its colorful array&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:38.280
of creatures, unique customs, and inspired visuals&nbsp;
that proved to be a sci-fi feast for the eyes.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:42.180
But as outlandish as the world of&nbsp;
Pandora appears on the surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:47.220
Cameron and company knew how to ground&nbsp;
it in reality to have audiences identify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:51.660
more with the motion-captured heroes and&nbsp;
reflect on issues within our own society.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.860
Sure, the subtlety of many of its&nbsp;
discrimination and environmental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:00.480
allegories are as subtle as a "Captain&nbsp;
Planet" cartoon at times, but nevertheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.500
it gave something for viewers to chew&nbsp;
on after the over three-hour runtime.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.460
So when diving into the film's&nbsp;
long-awaited sequel, "Avatar:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:13.740
The Way of Water," that social commentary would&nbsp;
undoubtedly be coming along with it, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:18.780
with the social and political changes the world&nbsp;
has seen in the 13 years between releases.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:24.000
While the environmental and imperialism&nbsp;
metaphors are here once again, perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.080
more crucial to "The Way of Water"&nbsp;
is something deeper and more timely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.860
in examining how one's identity&nbsp;
plays into the greater conflict.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:37.080
"Avatar: The Way of Water" introduces Jake&nbsp;
Sully and Neytiri's biological children&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:42.540
and their two adopted children Kiri and&nbsp;
Spider. The majority of the film explores&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:46.959
the kids and their place in the ongoing&nbsp;
conflict between the Na'vi and humans.

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:49.996
"I need you with me."

00:01:49.996 --> 00:01:55.080
While Sully's biological children constantly&nbsp;
try to prove themselves as great fighters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:59.940
like their parents, it is Kiri and Spider&nbsp;
who arguably have the greatest dilemma.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:06.480
Kiri is the biological daughter of the deceased&nbsp;
Dr. Grace Augustine, who, although naturally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:12.840
born as a Na'vi from Augustine's avatar, remains&nbsp;
conflicted over her place in Na'vi society while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:18.000
yearning to know more about her mother. Spider&nbsp;
sees a similar scenario in that he's the son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.120
of the film's antagonist, Colonel Miles Quaritch,&nbsp;
despite growing up with and identifying as Na'vi.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:29.160
But as he is forced to tag along with Quaritch&nbsp;
for the majority of the film to help locate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:34.260
Sully and his family, he remains conflicted&nbsp;
on which side to stay loyal to. Together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:38.520
both characters mirror one another as they&nbsp;
struggle with their identities while trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:43.140
to figure out where they fit in as Na'vi&nbsp;
people, or if they do at all. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:48.780
how these characters see themselves individually&nbsp;
makes its way into the grander story being told.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:53.220
Just as the first film comments on our&nbsp;
treatment of the forest landscapes around us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:57.840
it's probably not rocket science to figure&nbsp;
out what natural environment "Avatar:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.160
The Way of Water" chooses to highlight.&nbsp;
But the film's examination of how we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:07.380
should protect our seas also blends with&nbsp;
its themes about identity. Kiri is perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:12.420
the most prominent example of this, as the&nbsp;
teenager is established early on as having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:17.280
an especially close bond with the natural&nbsp;
world around her that's shrouded in mystery.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:22.260
When she and Sully's family make it to the&nbsp;
Metkayina tribe and begin learning their ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:27.780
Kiri is once again at one with her environment,&nbsp;
connecting with the sea creatures and world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:32.100
profoundly. Her abilities come in handy&nbsp;
towards the end, where she communicates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:36.600
with the sea life to help save her family&nbsp;
from certain death in Quaritch's ship.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:41.460
Her love for the environment around her&nbsp;
teaches us how to respect this resource&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:46.500
better and how the Na'vi people can learn&nbsp;
from her to settle this conflict. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:50.640
Spider's love for both the Na'vi&nbsp;
and his antagonistic father could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:53.580
help change the viewpoints of&nbsp;
everyone involved in the war.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:58.140
In the end, James Cameron's film acts as&nbsp;
more than a simple environmental message,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:02.520
but an examination of using your&nbsp;
unique viewpoint of the world to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.640
fight for what's right and how&nbsp;
that can help turn the tide.

